<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lapita pottery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lapita pottery is the dentate-stamped earthenware that defines the Lapita culture of the western Pacific, which appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> about 1500 BCE. Low-fired and tempered with shell or sand, it bears fine geometric patterns pressed into the clay with toothed stamps and closely resembles pottery from Nagsabaran in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. First found on Watom Island in 1909 and named after a site in New Caledonia excavated in 1952, it is known from more than 200 sites between the Bismarck Archipelago and Samoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lapita-pottery.jpg" alt="Lapita pottery" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torbenbrinker · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PortVilaLapita.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Lapita pottery was typically low-fired earthenware, tempered with shells or sand and decorated with a toothed stamp. Its makers used a variety of materials and techniques, depending on what was available. It has been suggested that the designs were transferred to clay from less durable materials such as bark cloth, mats or tattoos, or the other way round. Besides decorated ware the Lapita made undecorated beakers, cooking pots and bowls. In 2003 a cemetery was discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teouma">Teouma</a> on Efate Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, with 25 graves containing burial jars and 36 skeletons, all headless: the skulls had been removed after burial and replaced with rings made from cone shells. Carbon dating of the shells places the cemetery's use around 1000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Lapita people are believed to have come from the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and their pottery resembles pottery from the Nagsabaran site on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> more than any found elsewhere. In 2011 Peter Bellwood proposed that migrants moved east from the northern Philippines into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> and then south into the Bismarcks, possibly with others taking different routes. On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> the Lapita complex appears suddenly, as a fully developed horizon with no earlier stages, which suggests that a migrating population brought it.</p><p>The orthodox view, the Triple-I model of intrusion, innovation and integration, holds that Austronesian peoples moved into Near Oceania, developed new technologies there and merged with the existing populations. In 2016 DNA from four Lapita skeletons found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> showed descent from inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><p>Artifacts with Lapita designs later than 1,200 BCE have been found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> and New Caledonia, and Lapita pottery styles from around 1000 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and Western Polynesia. In Western Polynesia the pottery became less decorative and simpler over time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The first recorded discovery was in 1909, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-heart">Sacred Heart</a> missionary Otto Meyer found potsherds on Watom Island exposed by a tropical storm. In 1920 William C. McKern unearthed over 1,500 potsherds on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>, and the connection between the two finds was made in 1940 with pottery from the Ile des Pins. In 1952 Edward W. Gifford and Richard Shutler Jr. excavated in New Caledonia. Archaeologists misheard a word of the local Haveke language, xapeta'a, meaning to dig a hole, and coined the name Lapita. More than 200 Lapita sites have since been found, ranging over 4,000 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-archipelago">Bismarck Archipelago</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, with Samoa as the eastern limit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lapita culture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Lapita pottery</b> appears in the Bismarck Archipelago, north-east of New Guinea, between about 1500 and 1300 BCE; the exact date is still debated, and several recent studies favour the later end of that range.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…genetics show that their ancestry, as part of the wider Austronesian peoples, goes back about 5,000 years to the Indigenous peoples of Taiwan, whose descendants moved through the Philippines, along the edge of Melanesia, where they left <b>Lapita pottery</b>, and across Polynesia to New Zealand last of all.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The motifs of <b>Lapita pottery</b> were repeated with striking consistency from the Bismarcks to Samoa.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li></ul></section>
